# A small example

```java
package learning.tasks;
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@SpringBootApplication
public class TasksApplication {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    SpringApplication.run(TasksApplication.class, args);
  }
}
// Put this class in its own file, under learning.tasks.
@RestController
class PingController {
  @GetMapping("/ping")
  String ping() { return "pong"; }
}
```

```properties
# application.properties: change just this setting to try another port.
server.port=8081
```

Run the supplied Spring sandbox with Maven. Request GET /ping. Read the startup log and find the actual port. Change the port with --server.port=8082 and predict which value wins.

# A small example

```java
interface Notifier { void send(String text); }
@org.springframework.stereotype.Component
class ConsoleNotifier implements Notifier {
  public void send(String text) { System.out.println(text); }
}
@org.springframework.stereotype.Service
class TaskService {
  private final Notifier notifier;
  TaskService(Notifier notifier) { this.notifier = notifier; }
  void complete(long taskId) {
    notifier.send("Completed " + taskId);
  }
}
```

Test without Spring: new TaskService(text -> captured.add(text)).complete(7). Use an in-memory list owned by that test only.

# A small example

```java
record CreateTask(
  @jakarta.validation.constraints.NotBlank String title) {}
record TaskView(long id, String title) {}

@org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController
class TasksController {
  private final TaskCreator creator;
  TasksController(TaskCreator creator) { this.creator = creator; }
  @org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping("/tasks")
  org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity<TaskView> create(
    @jakarta.validation.Valid
    @org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestBody CreateTask input) {
    TaskView task = creator.create(input.title());
    return org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity
      .created(java.net.URI.create("/tasks/" + task.id())).body(task);
  }
}
// TaskCreator is the service boundary; see the runnable sandbox.
```

The validation starter must be installed. @Valid without a validation provider is not a substitute for the dependency.

# A small example

```java
// A framework-independent ownership rule, easy to unit test.
class TaskAccess {
  void requireOwner(long callerId, long ownerId) {
    if (callerId != ownerId) throw new org.springframework.security.access.AccessDeniedException("Forbidden");
  }
}
// In a use case: derive callerId from trusted authentication,
// load the task, check ownership, then change it.
// Never take callerId from an editable JSON field as proof of identity.
```

Configure a SecurityFilterChain bean for routes and authentication. This fragment deliberately does not pretend to be a complete production login system. In the sandbox, test a denied request as carefully as an allowed one.

# A small example

```java
@jakarta.persistence.Entity
class Task {
  @jakarta.persistence.Id
  @jakarta.persistence.GeneratedValue
  Long id;
  String title;
  @jakarta.persistence.ManyToOne(fetch = jakarta.persistence.FetchType.LAZY)
  Owner owner;
  protected Task() {}
}
// Owner is a separate entity with an id and name.
interface TaskRepository extends
    org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository<Task, Long> {
  @org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query(
    "select t from Task t join fetch t.owner where t.id > :after")
  java.util.List<Task> pageWithOwners(@org.springframework.data.repository.query.Param("after") long after);
}
```

This query illustrates a to-one fetch join. It is not a bounded production page: add a limit/Pageable and an explicit order before serving large lists. Collection fetch joins with pagination need special care.

# A small example

```java
@org.springframework.stereotype.Service
class CompleteTask {
  private final TaskRepository tasks;
  CompleteTask(TaskRepository tasks) { this.tasks = tasks; }
  @org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional
  public void run(long id) {
    Task task = tasks.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    task.title = "done"; // Example managed-field change.
    // Writing a separate outbox row here can make delivery intent atomic.
    // A later worker sends the external message with an idempotency key.
  }
}
```

Call run through an injected CompleteTask bean. For inventory, prefer an atomic conditional SQL update such as UPDATE stock SET quantity=quantity-1 WHERE id=? AND quantity>0. Check the affected-row count.

# A small example

```java
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;
class AccessTest {
  @org.junit.jupiter.api.Test
  void a_different_owner_cannot_edit() {
    TaskAccess access = new TaskAccess();
    assertThrows(org.springframework.security.access.AccessDeniedException.class,
      () -> access.requireOwner(3, 9));
  }
}
```

For HTTP use MockMvc with the appropriate Boot test configuration. For a full HTTP smoke test use @SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT). Boot 4 moved several test annotations to new packages: use the pinned sample and matching reference docs rather than pasting old imports.

## Follow a concrete example

```java
@org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties("receipts")
@org.springframework.validation.annotation.Validated
record ReceiptSettings(
  @jakarta.validation.constraints.NotNull java.net.URI endpoint,
  @jakarta.validation.constraints.Min(1) int maxAttempts) {}
// Enable with @ConfigurationPropertiesScan or @EnableConfigurationProperties.
```
```properties
receipts.endpoint=https://example.invalid/receipts
receipts.max-attempts=3
```

Use a clearly fake endpoint in a training environment. Do not send requests to it as if it were a real provider.

## Follow a concrete example

```java
interface ReceiptGateway {
  ReceiptResult send(String eventKey, ReceiptRequest request);
}
// Adapter responsibilities:
// configured timeouts; bounded retries; response validation;
// stable provider idempotency key if supported;
// map accepted/rejected/unknown outcomes explicitly.
```

In a fake adapter, record acceptance and then throw a timeout. This reproduces the difficult case without sending real messages.

# A small example

```js
// A tiny browser experiment, not a framework router replacement.
const button = document.querySelector('#open-tasks')
button?.addEventListener('click', () => {
  history.pushState({}, '', '/tasks')
  document.querySelector('main').textContent = 'Task list'
})
window.addEventListener('popstate', () => {
  console.log('Back/forward URL:', location.pathname)
})
```

Predict which events need a fresh document request. Use the Network panel with synthetic pages. pushState changes the URL; it does not fetch or render a route for you.

## Follow a concrete example

```js
const api = { retries: 0, muted: false, note: null }
const retries = api.retries ?? 3 // preserves 0
const muted = api.muted ?? true // preserves false

const saved = { title: 'Export fails', tags: ['billing'] }
const draft = { ...saved, tags: [...saved.tags] }
draft.tags.push('urgent') // does not change saved.tags
```

For a PATCH request, omit note to leave it alone; send note:null only if the server contract means “clear.” Do not invent that meaning on the client alone.

## Follow a concrete example

```js
function counter() {
  let value = 0
  return () => ++value
}
const a = counter(), b = counter()
a() // 1
a() // 2
b() // 1: separate closure

const formatter = { prefix: 'Ticket', label(id) { return `${this.prefix} ${id}` } }
const detached = formatter.label
const safe = formatter.label.bind(formatter)
safe(4) // 'Ticket 4'
// detached(4) throws when this is undefined in strict mode.
```

A timer callback reading a mutable selectedId will see its later value. Pass the intended ID as a local argument if the operation should belong to the original selection.

# A small example

```js
console.log('A')
setTimeout(() => console.log('B'), 0)
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('C'))
console.log('D')
// A, D, C, B

let latest = 0
async function search(query) {
  const request = ++latest
  const response = await fetch('/api/search?q=' + encodeURIComponent(query))
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Search failed')
  const rows = await response.json()
  if (request === latest) showRows(rows)
}
// The surrounding UI must also handle loading/errors per request.
```

